linscp (1.0-5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The client now reads a Wayland session as the desktop it is, instead of
    asking X11 alone. The window "terminal" opens was already refused when
    neither DISPLAY nor WAYLAND_DISPLAY was set, but which terminal was
    started afterwards did not depend on the session at all: a Wayland session
    whose x-terminal-emulator points at xterm got an X11 program, and an X11
    session with foot installed got a program that exits before it maps
    anything - the client said "Opened a terminal" and no window ever
    appeared. Terminals are now classified by the display server they can draw
    on, the compositor is found by its socket in XDG_RUNTIME_DIR (which is how
    a session started by a launcher or a user service is still found, and the
    inferred WAYLAND_DISPLAY is exported to the child), and a window that only
    XWayland can show is announced rather than chosen quietly.
  * The graphical client can ask the desktop for its window: linscp-gui
    --open-browser opens the served address through $BROWSER, xdg-open or
    gio - the URI launcher that works on both display servers - instead of
    leaving the user to copy the printed address, and it says so when there is
    no session to open a window in. linscp.desktop starts it that way, drops
    the startup notification it could never complete (the launcher maps no
    window) and gained TryExec; the package recommends xdg-utils, which is
    where xdg-open comes from, and prints the address instead when the user
    chose not to install it.
  * tools/check_wayland_compatibility.py is the guard. It refuses a
    display-session decision that reads DISPLAY with no WAYLAND_DISPLAY in
    view, a terminal candidate that does not say what it draws on, an X11-only
    or Wayland-only helper program, a desktop entry a compositor cannot act
    on, documentation of the terminal choice that never mentions Wayland, and
    the console and graphical detections disagreeing. It runs in "make -C
    source/linux check", as the CTest cases wayland-compatibility-check and
    wayland-compatibility-tests, and in CI, with its own regressions in
    tools/tests/test_wayland_compatibility.py.

 -- nuipona <nuipona@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 19 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

linscp (1.0-4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The Windows-to-Linux conversion is complete, and the build now checks
    it. The script command splitter read a backslash as the start of an
    escape inside double quotes - the rule of a Windows command line, which
    this client inherited with it. A path carried over from a Windows
    client lost its separators: "C:\Users\me" was read as "C:Usersme", and
    a file named "a\b.txt" as "ab.txt", so the name of one file became the
    name of another. The splitter follows the rules of a POSIX shell now,
    which is what the names on this platform are.
  * The error messages carried the operation phrases of the Windows client
    inside a template that supplies its own preposition, so the client
    said "Local change of directory to of '...' failed.". The phrases fit
    the template now, in English and in Spanish.
  * The public C ABI header of the Windows automation API
    (source/linux/liblinscp.h) is gone: it declared a library that no
    build produced, and the Makefile installed it. The automation object
    model stays a tracked gap.
  * The half-translated Nautilus file-manager extension is gone: it ran no
    session, so it uploaded nothing, and it was neither installed nor
    documented. The desktop extension stays a tracked gap.
  * The Makefile, CMake and the Debian package install the same files now.
    The graphical client's command templates are installed again - an
    installed client started with an empty command list - and the
    PowerShell module is in the package as well.
  * tools/check_platform_conversion.py reads the sources and refuses a
    Windows API call, a Windows environment variable, a Windows path, a
    file of another platform, an installer that disagrees with the others,
    and an error message that contradicts its template. It runs in
    "make -C source/linux check", as the CTest case
    "platform-conversion-check", and in CI.

 -- nuipona <nuipona@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

linscp (1.0-3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Test dependencies: the browser test suites use jsdom 29, the newest
    release that runs on the Node 20.19 in Debian 13, and CI checks out
    with actions/checkout v7.0.1.
  * The supported distribution is now Debian 13 (trixie) or newer: Debian
    12 ships Node 18, which no current jsdom release supports.
  * Declare conformance with Debian Policy 4.7.4; no package changes were
    required by the versions between 4.6.2 and 4.7.4.

 -- nuipona <nuipona@users.noreply.github.com>  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

linscp (1.0-2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Linux only: the Windows port is gone. The console client, the GUI and
    the test suites no longer carry a second platform's code paths, and the
    build is the Makefile, CMake/CTest and this package.
  * SFTP always uses the bundled PuTTY core; there is no curl fallback for
    it, and the curl-backed protocols say so when the installed curl lacks
    the protocol rather than naming a platform.
  * Install the graphical client properly: /usr/bin/linscp-gui is now a
    launcher, and the Python modules (linscp_gui.py, linscp_crypto.py) and
    the web client are installed under /usr/share/linscp/gui. The previous
    package copied the server script alone into /usr/bin, where it could not
    import its own crypto module, so the packaged GUI did not start.
  * Recommend curl, which every protocol except SFTP needs at run time.

 -- nuipona <nuipona@users.noreply.github.com>  Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:40:00 -0800

linscp (1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Native POSIX port of the LinSCP command-line client.
  * Builds linscp(1) from the shared PuTTY core, the vendored upstream
    PuTTY 0.85 POSIX platform layer and the front end in source/linux.
  * SFTP sessions: connect, host key checking, ssh-agent, public key and
    password authentication, and the open/cd/pwd/ls/lcd/lpwd/get/put/rm/
    rmdir/mkdir/mv/chmod/stat/option/help/exit commands.

 -- nuipona <nuipona@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 12 Oct 2026 08:00:00 +0000
